{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>To commit harlotry with the daughters of Moab</b> — through the counsel of Balaam, as is stated in the chapter (Sanhedrin 106a); this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 25:1). And in truth, the harlotry was not the scheme of the women, for it was done through the counsel of their men and their leaders, and from the elders of Midian they had guidance, as it is stated concerning the Midianites: \"For they are enemies to you in their wiles, which they schemed against you in the matter of Peor\" (Numbers 25:18), for they schemed against them with a plot to make them stray after Baal Peor. And therefore it is possible that Balaam was in the counsel, for he was the greatest of their advisers, and his intent was to do evil to Israel, and he did everything in his power in this matter, as it is stated: \"And HaShem your Elohim refused to listen to Balaam\" (Deuteronomy 23:6), and therefore they killed him by the sword. But according to the plain sense, Scripture did not mention the counsel here, but only in what it says afterward: \"Behold, these were to the children of Israel, through the matter of Balaam\" (Numbers 31:16), just as it did not mention the counsel of the elders of Midian except in what it said: \"For they are enemies to you.\" After the fact, at the time of vengeance, it mentions whence the evil came to them, and says that the deed was not from the impulse of lust that is in men and women by their nature from their youth, but rather an evil counsel to make them stray. And therefore great vengeance was deserved. For the meaning of \"Come, I will advise you what this people shall do to your people in the end of days\" is indeed as I have explained (Ramban on Numbers 24:14). And it is further possible according to the plain sense that Balak at first desired to curse them and to wage war against them, and would not let them enter his territory at all. And when Balaam told him that he could not prevail against them, and informed him that they would not destroy his land and his people except in the end of days, then he brought out bread and wine in the plains of Moab and lured them as though he loved them. And this is \"through the matter of Balaam,\" for on account of his words they did so. But because his desire was to curse them and he accepted payment from Balak for this, were it not for the righteous deeds of HaShem, who turned the curse into a blessing, therefore they killed him by the sword. For both the one who hires and the one who is hired were punished, as it is stated: \"And whom he hired against you Balaam the son of Beor,\" etc. (Deuteronomy 23:5)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The received text reads ה' (HaShem) in the Deuteronomy 23:6 citation, but the Masoretic Text of that verse reads יהוה; translated as HaShem following the manuscript reading given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:44.048142130Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}